{
  "instrument": {
    "state": "Treat a reconstructed applicant scheduling case as the configuration: a fixed application, required orientation and supervised session, applicant availability, instructor and equipment availability, and observed administrative or scheduling conditions that might be changed. These conditions are candidate removable parts; required instruction, safety checks and necessary maintenance remain fixed. First link existing step dates using the minimum identifying information needed, and prospectively record handoffs, contact attempts, prerequisites, offered slots and cancellations. Ask only access-arrangement questions, respect declined contact, and leave unexplained nonresponse unresolved. Before testing, choose a waiting-time threshold and decision horizon. Define failure as permission remaining unavailable beyond that threshold for a fixed applicant; do not improve the result by excluding the applicant or resetting the clock. Whether sufficiently reproducible cases and feasible removable conditions exist is initially unknown.",
    "operation": "Construct a scheduling replay for selected delayed cases, explicitly documenting assumptions about missing dates, availability and volunteer capacity. Require the unreduced replay to reproduce the documented delay; if it cannot, investigate missing information before reduction. Partition verified, changeable conditions, such as a measured handoff lag or a documented batching rule if one exists. Remove subsets or test their complements by substituting a specified feasible arrangement, while holding the applicant, required steps and other conditions fixed. Retain a reduced configuration only when repeated tests still reproduce the defined failure. Continue partitioning and testing until no single remaining condition can be removed while preserving failure, or until the decision deadline, unresolved outcomes or lack of feasible substitutions stops reduction. Record those stopping reasons. A deletion that lacks an operationally feasible counterpart is exploratory only and cannot justify changing arrangements.",
    "signal": "For each candidate reduction, record failure reproduced, failure absent, or unresolved, alongside predicted permission date, waiting between steps and the remaining blocking conditions. Unknown applicant availability, ambiguous nonresponse or an unsupported scheduling assumption makes the relevant outcome unresolved, not successful or unsuccessful. Repeat tests across plausible uncertain inputs; retain a reduction as robust only if its failure classification remains stable. Where applicants consent and volunteers can support it, test promising arrangements prospectively and compare observed handoff and completion times with replay predictions. No such observations are presumed to have occurred.",
    "inference": "A retained subset identifies conditions jointly sufficient to reproduce excessive waiting for that applicant under the specified replay and assumptions. If every single deletion has been tested and eliminates failure, it is minimal against those deletions, not necessarily the smallest possible subset. Compare reductions across current and prospective cases to identify recurring delays and how many applicants they affect. Then separately test feasible allocations of the two evening slots to orientation, equipment-specific supervision, or intake coordination where evidence indicates coordination is blocking progress. Test both slots together as well as individually, preserving instructor, equipment and maintenance capacity. Use predicted reductions in excessive waits, confirmed where possible by prospective results, to choose next month's allocation. A retained condition alone does not establish that adding a session will help. If evidence remains weak, make any allocation a limited monitored trial with a specified review date.",
    "limit": "Replaying the same assumptions repeatedly establishes computational repeatability, not real-world reproducibility. Historical reconstruction alone cannot establish the effects of changed arrangements. Interactions, cancellations, changing demand, applicant availability, missing records and the order of reductions can produce different retained subsets. Separate spreadsheets and monthly totals may support only partial reconstruction; do not force a minimal result from unresolved tests. Reductions from selected cases do not establish the largest source of delay across the queue, a unique cause, or guaranteed benefits from extra sessions. If prospective outcomes contradict predictions, revise the reconstruction and retest. Stop any trial that would compromise required instruction, safety checks or necessary maintenance; stop contacting an applicant who declines, and retain their unexplained delay as unknown rather than treating it as resolved."
  }
}
